Maze Pathfinder System - Prolog Implementation
Overview

This Prolog program implements a maze pathfinding system using Dijkstra's algorithm to find the shortest path between nodes while avoiding faulty nodes. The system is designed for maze navigation or network routing scenarios where some nodes may be inaccessible.
Features
    Shortest Path Calculation: Finds the optimal path between any two valid nodes

    Faulty Node Handling: Dynamically manages inaccessible nodes

    Error Checking: Validates node existence and accessibility

    Interactive Commands: Easy-to-use interface for pathfinding and system management


Requirements
    SWI-Prolog (version 8.2.0 or later recommended)
    Mazedata.pl file containing maze topology

Installation
    Place both this program file and your Mazedata.pl in the same directory


Loading the Program---->
['maze_solver.pl', 'Mazedata.pl'].

Available Commands

Path Finding--->
shortest_path(Start, End, Path).

    Finds and displays the shortest path from Start to End

    Example: shortest_path(0, 70, Path).

Faulty Node Management---->
add_faulty(Node).

    Marks a node as faulty/inaccessible
    Example: add_faulty(25).

remove_faulty(Node).

    Removes faulty status from a node
    Example: remove_faulty(25).



list_faulty.
    Lists all currently faulty nodes

cleanup.

    Resets the pathfinder's internal state

    Use after modifying faulty nodes or maze structure

help.

    Displays available commands

Examples

    Find a path while avoiding faulty nodes:

prolog
?- shortest_path(0, 70, Path).
Shortest path: 0 -> 1 -> 11 -> 21 -> 22 -> 32 -> 42 -> 52 -> 62 -> 72 -> 71 -> 70

    Add a faulty node and find an alternative path:

prolog

?- add_faulty(22).
Marked node 22 as faulty.

?- shortest_path(0, 70, Path).
Shortest path: 0 -> 10 -> 20 -> 30 -> 31 -> 41 -> 51 -> 61 -> 71 -> 70

    List all faulty nodes:

prolog

?- list_faulty.
Faulty nodes:
